From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Domenico D'Arrigo <d.darrigo@outlook.it>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 23:28:03 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] UefiPayloadPkg: TCG Secure MOR with the lock that coreboot
 keeps in SMM

The variable driver without SMM (VariableRuntimeDxe) reports
MemoryOverwriteRequestControlLock as unsupported: a lock in its memory
is no lock against code in the OS, which is what revision 2 of the TCG
Platform Reset Attack Mitigation specification guards against. Windows
needs it for Credential Guard's protection of its secrets across a reset.

coreboot can keep the lock (SMMSTORE_CMD_MOR_LOCK, with SMMSTORE_MOR_LOCK):
in SMM, where the OS cannot reach it, it refuses the writes of the
variable store that would clear the request of the OS while the lock
holds, and it decides whether the lock is offered at all (only with the
flash written by SMM alone, BIOS Lock).

- MdeModulePkg: EDKII_PLATFORM_MOR_LOCK_PROTOCOL, the lock of a
  platform for the variable driver without SMM. TcgMorLockDxe.c, with
  PcdTcgMorWithoutSmm, the protocol and a MOR variable that the platform
  created, offers the lock to the OS as the SMM version does (lock, lock
  with a key, unlock with the key; a wrong key locks until the next boot;
  MOR refused while locked) and takes each lock and unlock through the
  protocol, which decides; the variable is set to 0 at EndOfDxe. Without
  any of the three, the driver does what it did.
- UefiPayloadPkg: SmmStoreLibMorLock(), and MorLockSmmStoreDxe, which
  installs the protocol when coreboot keeps the lock in this boot.

TEST=gcc -fsyntax-only -Wall -Wextra -Werror of TcgMorLockDxe.c,
MorLockSmmStoreDxe.c and SmmStore.c with the EDK2 headers, debug and
MDEPKG_NDEBUG.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
